# Turn discipline in long autonomous runs

Unscoped on purpose: this has to be loaded in the sessions it governs, which are
ordinary working sessions, not prompt-authoring ones.

Measured over a month of transcripts: 102 turns ended by announcing a
continuation and then stopping, and 19 more ended asking about a `git push`
already authorized.

A turn ends when the work does, not when a report is ready. Never close a turn
on "next I will" or by naming the remaining item: if the next action is known
and safe, take it in the same turn. Report status in a brief note between tool
calls, and when you checkpoint, say what happened and continue rather than
offering a menu.

Reuse an authorization already on record - in instructions, project files, or
memory - when its recorded scope covers what you are about to do: same
repository, same kind of action, same branch or target. A one-off approval, or a
record whose scope is unclear, has expired. Do not re-ask for permission whose
scope plainly still applies, and do not stretch one grant to cover a different
target.
